  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks made atomic spectroscopy an exceptionally precise and stable measurement platform. Katori and Ye made distinct contributions to the concept and its realization. Independent development, sustained improvements, and reproducibility establish a mature achievement whose value includes tests of fundamental physics as well as timekeeping.",
    "case_against": "Clock performance continues to improve, and later advances involved a broad community. The prize contribution must be framed around the foundational lattice-clock advances without treating every subsequent precision milestone as part of the discovery.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson ranked optical lattice clocks fifth, recognizing their accuracy and maturity while asking how much weight to give the continuing evolution of clock performance.",
        "response": "That is a real boundary question. I would ground the prize in the new measurement regime established by the lattice clocks and their demonstrated precision, not in the latest performance record. Continuing refinement strengthens the evidence for the platform without changing the central achievement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasized that Katori and Ye made distinct contributions and that the clocks are mature, while noting the broader community's role in later performance and applications.",
        "response": "I agree that the two independent contributions form a strong basis for attribution. The wider community's work should inform the scope of the citation, but it does not erase the foundational advances that made these clocks possible."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The precise boundary between the foundational lattice-clock achievement and later clock-development contributions needs careful definition."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
